1
00:00:09,230 --> 00:00:10,830
I've never seen him this happy before.

2
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:11,890
That's because you've never seen him

3
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:14,520
on "Restock the Medicine Cabinet Day."

4
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:15,920
Look!

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,610
A new topical antihistamine with lidocaine.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:22,710
I can't wait until I get a rash.

7
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:27,290
Oh, Gas-X has a new ultra strength.

8
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:29,460
I guess they really do read their mail.

9
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:34,100
Hey, isn't that Professor Proton?

10
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:35,470
Oh, yeah.

11
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:41,930
Look at him, just standing in line

12
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:45,240
like he wasn't moderately famous 30 years ago.

13
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:47,020
Let's go say hello.

14
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,070
Oh, maybe we shouldn't bother him.

15
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:51,690
I'm not going to bother him, I'm going to talk to him.

16
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:55,340
He thinks there's a difference.

17
00:00:55,530 --> 00:00:56,530
Arthur?

18
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,320
Arthur, it's me--

19
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,580
Sheldon Cooper.

20
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:03,120
You may not remember because of your advanced age.

21
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:06,780
Trust me, I... I remember.

22
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,980
This is my girlfriend, Amy.

23
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:11,660
Amy, this is television legend Arthur Jeffries.

24
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:15,390
His science show inspired millions of children.

25
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:17,440
Ho-hold on.

26
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,350
You... You have a girlfriend?

27
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,700
Yes, and I've heard so much about you.

28
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:27,420
Hey look-- we're wearing the same orthopedic shoes.

29
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,640
I can't believe I dress like a celebrity.

30
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:35,550
O-Okay, I get it now.

31
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:39,270
Dr. Jeffries, hello again. Leonard Hofstadter.

32
00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:42,610
Oh, oh, right, I remember your-your girlfriend.

33
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:47,260
Is-Is she... is she here?

34
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:49,940
No, she's not.

35
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:51,990
So, what prescription are you getting filled?

36
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:53,790
- Sheldon. - Wait, I want to guess.

37
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:55,250
Don't tell me.

38
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:58,050
I wasn't going to tell you.

39
00:01:58,450 --> 00:01:59,510
Sheldon, come on.

40
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:00,730
No, no, no. I'm really good at this.

41
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:01,670
All right, give me a hint.

42
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:06,640
Does it involve difficulty initiating a stream of anything?

43
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:12,630
Well, given my age, that's more than just a lucky guess.

44
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,320
Hey, Sheldon, let's go mock the people

45
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:17,070
buying homeopathic medicine-- you love that.

46
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:18,670
But I'm hanging out with my friend,

47
00:02:18,670 --> 00:02:19,920
and we're having fun.

48
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:22,530
Look how happy he is.

49
00:02:51,510 --> 00:02:54,690
Bernie's having a girls' night on Friday at our place.

50
00:02:54,690 --> 00:02:56,050
You want to do something?

51
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,600
- Actually, I'm busy. - Doing what?

52
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,020
There's a new sports bar over on Colorado Ave...

53
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:02,530
- You're going to girls' night. - Yeah.

54
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:07,440
You know they're making jewelry, right?

55
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:09,810
You think they came up with that?

56
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:12,930
They were going to drink beer and play darts.

57
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:14,920
What's up?

58
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:17,370
Not his testosterone levels.

59
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:18,320
Excuse me.

60
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,420
I happen to be very comfortable with my masculinity.

61
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:23,780
How is that possible?

62
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:31,110
Hey, I got an email from Professor Proton.

63
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:32,620
Goody! What's it say?

64
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:35,440
He's working on a paper about nano vacuum tubes,

65
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:37,920
and wants to know if I'd take a look at it.

66
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,850
That's strange.

67
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:42,550
That he would come to you for that and not me.

68
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:46,200
Oh, I got two emails from him.

69
00:03:46,970 --> 00:03:50,020
"Do you have Leonard's email address?"

70
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:53,910
And...

71
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:56,600
"Never mind, I found it."

72
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:00,490
I can't believe he picked you over me.

73
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:02,800
You don't want to read a paper by some old has-been

74
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,160
who hasn't done any real science in decades.

75
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:08,040
Yeah, it's nothing to cry about.

76
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:10,650
Sheldon Cooper does not cry.

77
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:13,220
It's true, you'd rust.

78
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:20,920
And to think I idolized that man.

79
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,280
And why?

80
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:25,350
At the end of the day he's just another Hollywood phony.

81
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:28,620
Is it really worth getting upset about?

82
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:30,510
They say don't meet your heroes.

83
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:33,510
Don't peek behind that curtain of fame and celebrity,

84
00:04:33,510 --> 00:04:36,390
because if you do, you'll see them as they really are--

85
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:38,670
degenerate carnival folk.

86
00:04:40,350 --> 00:04:42,630
Come on, he's a retired kids show host.

87
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:43,800
That's even worse.

88
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:46,010
Using the sweet candy of science

89
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:48,130
to trick children into loving him.

90
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:50,740
Pervert.

91
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:54,570
Have you ever thought about

92
00:04:54,570 --> 00:04:57,300
why Arthur didn't want you to read his paper?

93
00:04:57,310 --> 00:04:58,710
Yes, I have.

94
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:00,040
And my only conclusion

95
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:01,910
is the prescription he was picking up the other day

96
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:03,770
was for cuckoo pills.

97
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:08,320
Maybe he found you, um, a bit much.

98
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:11,660
That's kind of a stretch.

99
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,310
Look, when it comes to social skills,

100
00:05:13,310 --> 00:05:14,760
I've mastered the big three.

101
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:16,890
There's the coy smile.

102
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:20,650
There's the friendly chuckle.

103
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,850
There's the vocalization of sympathy.

104
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:31,590
That last one's tricky, I'm still working on it.

105
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:34,280
From what I saw the other day,

106
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:36,920
I could understand why he and...

107
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:40,100
some people might find you...

108
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:43,280
What?

109
00:05:45,010 --> 00:05:46,340
Doesn't matter.

110
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:49,430
No, go ahead, say it.

111
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:50,930
I know what it is.

112
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:52,370
I've heard it my whole life.

113
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:53,560
The word's "annoying".

114
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:54,870
Go ahead, say it.

115
00:05:54,870 --> 00:05:56,860
Say it. Say I'm annoying.

116
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:57,580
Sheldon...

117
00:05:57,580 --> 00:05:59,200
Oh, it won't hurt my feelings.

118
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,030
Go ahead, Amy, say I'm annoying.

119
00:06:01,030 --> 00:06:03,500
I'm annoying. I'm annoying.

120
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:04,800
I'm annoying. Yeah, now, uh...

121
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:05,730
Now where are you going?

122
00:06:05,730 --> 00:06:08,200
Look, you know you want to say it-- say it.

123
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,690
Say I'm annoying. Go ahead, say it.

124
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:14,390
Say it. Say it. Amy, say it.

125
00:06:16,990 --> 00:06:19,350
Well, she can't stand it when I'm right.

126
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:22,690
This is fun.

127
00:06:22,690 --> 00:06:24,460
I'm going to feel like such a vixen wearing jewelry

128
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:27,520
that doesn't have a list of medications I'm allergic to.

129
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:30,000
Penny, how's it going over there?

130
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,960
Good-- I'm just having little trouble with the glue.

131
00:06:34,590 --> 00:06:36,330
How do you not know how to use glue?

132
00:06:36,330 --> 00:06:38,430
Did you ditch pre-school?

133
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:42,090
Yeah, but only because I was dating a second grader.

134
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:45,900
Hello, everyone.

135
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,290
Okay, let me have it.

136
00:06:48,290 --> 00:06:51,260
Let's hear all the "Raj is a girl" jokes.

137
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:51,920
Nope.

138
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:54,610
Bernadette told me it isn't nice and I'm not allowed.

139
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:55,300
Thank you.

140
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:56,760
So I won't be making fun of you,

141
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:57,970
or the things you like,

142
00:06:57,970 --> 00:07:01,380
or the fact that you just want to have fun.

143
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:05,740
Howie, stop.

144
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:07,590
Come on, look at what I'm making.

145
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:09,860
Oh, that's actually pretty nice.

146
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:11,020
I'm making a bracelet.

147
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:13,160
Yeah, I'm just making a mess.

148
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,940
You know, instead of beads and glue,

149
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:19,340
you guys can use my soldering iron.

150
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:21,140
You'd be able to make much cooler stuff.

151
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:24,710
Oh, I think we're doing just fine, thank you.

152
00:07:24,710 --> 00:07:27,420
- Actually, I'd kind of like to try that. - Me, too.

153
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:29,270
Be right back.

154
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:32,540
When did I have pistachios?

155
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:38,970
Arthur.

156
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:40,680
Arthur.

157
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:42,700
Arthur.

158
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:45,710
Door knocker.

159
00:07:45,710 --> 00:07:47,930
That's TV money.

160
00:07:51,050 --> 00:07:52,730
Sheldon, what are you doing here?

161
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:55,450
I'm sorry, did I wake you?

162
00:07:55,450 --> 00:07:58,090
Of course you woke me, it's 7:30.

163
00:08:00,290 --> 00:08:01,850
Well, I would have been here sooner,

164
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:03,310
but for some reason your home

165
00:08:03,310 --> 00:08:06,510
isn't on this map of Hollywood stars.

166
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,890
What do you want, Sheldon?

167
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:11,910
It's been pointed out by my girlfriend

168
00:08:11,910 --> 00:08:14,440
that I may have been annoying to you.

169
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:16,900
She sounds like a keeper.

170
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,600
Anyway, I wanted to apologize.

171
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:22,070
I am truly sorry.

172
00:08:22,070 --> 00:08:24,270
All right, apology accepted. Have a nice night.

173
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:27,050
No, no, now that... we've cleared the air,

174
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:29,740
I wanted to discuss another matter with you.

175
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:32,440
Sheldon, in a couple hours, I have to get up,

176
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:35,770
pee and then wander around the house.

177
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:43,360
Well, then, I'll make this quick.

178
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:46,940
Um, I want to talk about the paper you sent Leonard.

179
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:48,810
Now, I know he said it's promising

180
00:08:48,810 --> 00:08:50,700
and he's going to collaborate on it,

181
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:53,030
but now that you and I are friends again,

182
00:08:53,030 --> 00:08:54,660
I am at your disposal.

183
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:57,680
Frankly, lending my name and reputation to it will help,

184
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:01,150
because a lot of people think that you're a washed-up has-been.

185
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,590
Or dead.

186
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:06,910
I should be so lucky.

187
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:10,650
So, uh, what do you say?

188
00:09:10,650 --> 00:09:12,080
You know, if it's all the same with you,

189
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:14,230
I think I'll stick with Leonard.

190
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:16,360
It's because I'm annoying, right?

191
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,300
I know it is. Say it.

192
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:20,020
Say it. Say I'm annoying.

193
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:21,430
- Good night. - Say it.

194
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:23,470
Arthur, say it. Say I'm annoying.

195
00:09:23,470 --> 00:09:25,250
Say it. Say it.

196
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:27,320
I'm annoying. Say it.

197
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:30,280
You're annoying!

198
00:09:33,490 --> 00:09:35,450
Wow, that really hurt.

199
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,040
If you would have told me when I was a kid

200
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:43,580
that someday I would be doing science

201
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:45,080
next to Professor Proton,

202
00:09:45,080 --> 00:09:47,200
I would not have believed you.

203
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:48,240
If someone had told me

204
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,740
that people would still call me Professor Proton

205
00:09:50,740 --> 00:09:53,020
when I was 83 years old,

206
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:55,880
I never would have quit smoking.

207
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:01,070
I'm sorry, Dr. Jeffries, I'm just excited.

208
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:04,060
This nano vacuum tube idea of yours is really interesting.

209
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:05,120
Hello, Leonard.

210
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:06,860
Oh, hello, Arthur.

211
00:10:06,860 --> 00:10:09,510
What an odd coincidence that you're both here.

212
00:10:09,510 --> 00:10:10,940
Why is it odd?

213
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:13,730
Because as it just so happens, I'm also spending the day

214
00:10:13,730 --> 00:10:16,860
with a beloved children's television science personality.

215
00:10:17,070 --> 00:10:19,290
Isn't that right, new friend and colleague,

216
00:10:19,290 --> 00:10:21,540
Bill Nye the Science Guy.

217
00:10:25,810 --> 00:10:28,860
Sorry, I replaced you with a newer model.

218
00:10:29,950 --> 00:10:31,610
Wow, Arthur Jeffries.

219
00:10:31,610 --> 00:10:32,980
It's an honor to meet you.

220
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:35,320
My show never would have happened without yours.

221
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:37,780
That's what I told my lawyers.

222
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:41,170
Mr. Nye, hello.

223
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:44,030
I'm sorry he got you involved in this nonsense.

224
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:46,330
He said I'd be speaking to a class.

225
00:10:46,330 --> 00:10:49,170
No, I said you were teaching someone a lesson.

226
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:50,420
Now let's go.

227
00:10:51,990 --> 00:10:53,210
What are you guys working on?

228
00:10:53,210 --> 00:10:55,430
Oh, uh, we're making nano vacuum tubes.

229
00:10:56,860 --> 00:10:58,190
That's interesting.

230
00:10:58,190 --> 00:10:59,630
Haven't you stolen enough from me?

231
00:10:59,630 --> 00:11:01,650
Back off, bow tie!

232
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:08,940
Hey, guys...

233
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:10,720
What do you think?

234
00:11:11,050 --> 00:11:12,690
Wow, Penny, good job.

235
00:11:12,690 --> 00:11:14,690
Thanks. Target, $4.99.

236
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:16,060
I'm getting a drink.

237
00:11:17,330 --> 00:11:18,410
What are you working on?

238
00:11:18,410 --> 00:11:20,240
Oh, I'm making a necklace for my mom.

239
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:21,220
That's sweet.

240
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:22,510
Yeah, she and my dad are going through

241
00:11:22,510 --> 00:11:23,870
a bit of a rough patch, so

242
00:11:23,870 --> 00:11:24,990
I wanted to do something to let her know

243
00:11:24,990 --> 00:11:26,280
I was thinking about her.

244
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:27,680
What's going on with them?

245
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:29,940
They're just having a little trouble communicating.

246
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:32,270
My dad says it's because the sound of my mom's voice

247
00:11:32,270 --> 00:11:33,610
makes him want to tear his ears off

248
00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:34,550
and sew them over his eyes

249
00:11:34,550 --> 00:11:36,300
so he never has to look at her again.

250
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:39,370
Hi.

251
00:11:39,370 --> 00:11:40,380
- Hey, guys. - Hello.

252
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:41,270
Look who's here to put

253
00:11:41,270 --> 00:11:43,970
the "Jew" In "Jewelry Night"!

254
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:49,800
Oh, sure, so it's fine when you say it.

255
00:11:50,690 --> 00:11:51,410
Sorry we're late.

256
00:11:51,410 --> 00:11:52,400
I wanted to swing by the lab

257
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:55,520
and pick up some even cooler tools for us to use.

258
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:56,880
I didn't know you were coming again.

259
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,250
Well, last week was a blast.

260
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:01,280
Plus, I thought you might like having a guy here.

261
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:06,660
It was not funny.

262
00:12:07,670 --> 00:12:09,090
So, what tools did you bring?

263
00:12:09,090 --> 00:12:10,870
Everything we need to make jewelry molds.

264
00:12:10,870 --> 00:12:12,620
Here's some silver, a crucible

265
00:12:12,620 --> 00:12:14,780
and an acetylene torch to melt it down.

266
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:16,650
Ooh, that looks like fun.

267
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:18,080
Maybe you should master glue

268
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:20,410
before you move on to fire.

269
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:22,810
Okay, who's up first?

270
00:12:22,810 --> 00:12:24,670
Rajesh, do you want to make a bracelet for your mom

271
00:12:24,670 --> 00:12:26,110
to go with the necklace?

272
00:12:26,110 --> 00:12:27,210
No, thanks.

273
00:12:27,270 --> 00:12:29,120
I was gonna make a necklace for my mom,

274
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,770
but unfortunately, she doesn't have a neck.

275
00:12:33,490 --> 00:12:36,860
She's just chins and fat and feet.

276
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:42,610
Okay, we're almost ready to go.

277
00:12:42,610 --> 00:12:45,090
Once we bond the wires, we can test the tubes.

278
00:12:45,090 --> 00:12:47,820
This is so exciting, I-I feel...

279
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:50,630
like I'm 75 again.

280
00:12:54,830 --> 00:12:56,210
Oh, God.

281
00:12:56,210 --> 00:12:57,350
What... what is it?

282
00:12:57,350 --> 00:12:58,500
Sheldon just sent me a picture

283
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:01,070
of him and Bill Nye getting smoothies.

284
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:05,830
Can-can I ask you a question?

285
00:13:05,830 --> 00:13:07,260
Yeah, sure.

286
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:10,050
W-Why do you put up with Sheldon?

287
00:13:10,050 --> 00:13:13,760
Oh, uh, you know, because we're friends.

288
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:16,990
Why?

289
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:21,740
- Wow, you ask really hard questions. - Yeah.

290
00:13:22,090 --> 00:13:24,630
Look, I-I know he can be aggravating,

291
00:13:24,630 --> 00:13:25,990
but what you have to remember is that

292
00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:27,430
he's not doing it on purpose.

293
00:13:27,430 --> 00:13:29,160
It's just how he is.

294
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:32,280
But he's also loyal and trustworthy

295
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:33,770
and we have fun together...

296
00:13:33,770 --> 00:13:36,980
You... you know you're describing a dog.

297
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:40,640
He did bite me once.

298
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,230
But in his defense,

299
00:13:43,230 --> 00:13:45,270
I came up behind him while he was eating, so...

300
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:48,430
Yeah, they-they hate that.

301
00:13:50,110 --> 00:13:53,990
You know what, Sheldon is the smartest person I have ever met.

302
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:57,610
And he's a little broken and he needs me.

303
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:00,630
I guess I need him, too.

304
00:14:01,150 --> 00:14:02,670
Why-why is that?

305
00:14:02,670 --> 00:14:05,080
Boy, you will not let this go, will you?

306
00:14:07,810 --> 00:14:09,150
Oh, geez.

307
00:14:09,150 --> 00:14:10,490
Another photo from Sheldon?

308
00:14:10,490 --> 00:14:12,080
No, I have to go pick him up.

309
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:14,550
Bill Nye ditched him at the smoothie place.

310
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:18,850
He probably stole his wallet, too.

311
00:14:23,550 --> 00:14:24,710
You know, if you guys are interested,

312
00:14:24,710 --> 00:14:27,060
there's a technique where I can take a lock of your hair,

313
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:28,980
refine it into carbon dust,

314
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:30,390
and use the hydraulic press at work

315
00:14:30,390 --> 00:14:32,320
to turn it into a tiny little diamond

316
00:14:32,320 --> 00:14:33,800
with your DNA in it.

317
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:35,570
Oh, that's amazing!

318
00:14:35,570 --> 00:14:36,850
Yeah. See Bernie's engagement ring?

319
00:14:36,850 --> 00:14:39,240
That came right off my mom's back.

320
00:14:40,940 --> 00:14:42,220
He's kidding.

321
00:14:42,220 --> 00:14:44,870
If that were true, it'd be so much bigger.

322
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:47,720
"My name is Howard.

323
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:49,110
I can make your hair into diamonds.

324
00:14:49,110 --> 00:14:50,390
My mom is morbidly obese.

325
00:14:50,390 --> 00:14:52,360
Everybody love me."

326
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:55,750
Whoa, where is that coming from?

327
00:14:55,750 --> 00:14:57,560
I'll tell you where it's coming from.

328
00:14:57,560 --> 00:14:59,570
All you do is make fun of me for coming to girls' night

329
00:14:59,570 --> 00:15:01,710
and now you're here ruining it for everyone.

330
00:15:01,710 --> 00:15:02,740
Raj, cool it...

331
00:15:02,740 --> 00:15:05,070
he's gonna make us hair diamonds.

332
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:07,500
How am I ruining anything?

333
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,770
I'm just trying to help you make better jewelry.

334
00:15:09,770 --> 00:15:11,410
But this isn't about the jewelry.

335
00:15:11,410 --> 00:15:13,050
This is about me having a place

336
00:15:13,050 --> 00:15:15,350
where I can open up about my feelings.

337
00:15:15,350 --> 00:15:18,470
Since when can't you open up with me?

338
00:15:18,470 --> 00:15:19,950
There are just some things

339
00:15:19,950 --> 00:15:21,810
that I feel more comfortable sharing with the girls,

340
00:15:21,810 --> 00:15:23,430
because they won't make fun of me

341
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:24,830
or and call me names, or ask me if my

342
00:15:24,830 --> 00:15:27,780
"Koothrapanties are in a bunch."

343
00:15:31,390 --> 00:15:34,210
Buddy, I was just joking around.

344
00:15:34,210 --> 00:15:36,530
Yeah, well, sometimes your jokes hurt.

345
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:38,380
Well, you're right.

346
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:41,910
I didn't realize I was making you feel that way.

347
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,100
It was very brave of you to tell me.

348
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:46,980
Thank you.

349
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:48,770
It wasn't easy.

350
00:15:50,470 --> 00:15:52,200
They're gonna have sex before Sheldon and I do,

351
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:54,280
I know it.

352
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:00,230
Arthur.

353
00:16:00,230 --> 00:16:01,680
Hi, Sheldon.

354
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:03,550
I'm surprised to see you here.

355
00:16:03,550 --> 00:16:04,860
Yeah, me, too.

356
00:16:04,860 --> 00:16:07,260
Somewhere around the third floor,

357
00:16:07,260 --> 00:16:10,260
I began to see a white light.

358
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:13,810
I'll get Leonard.

359
00:16:13,810 --> 00:16:14,940
No, no, no, I'm...

360
00:16:15,570 --> 00:16:17,920
I'm here... I'm here to see you.

361
00:16:17,920 --> 00:16:19,580
If you're hoping to get in touch with Bill Nye,

362
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:21,190
I can't help you.

363
00:16:21,710 --> 00:16:22,700
I've been informed that he's now

364
00:16:22,700 --> 00:16:25,530
Bill Nye the Restraining Order Guy.

365
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:28,470
No, I'm...

366
00:16:28,470 --> 00:16:31,080
I-I was thinking, if it isn't too much trouble,

367
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:35,670
I'd like to get your opinion about my-my paper.

368
00:16:35,670 --> 00:16:36,910
Thank you.

369
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:38,330
It would be an honor.

370
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:39,800
Oh. Uh, great.

371
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:41,470
Well, actually, I don't need that.

372
00:16:41,470 --> 00:16:44,470
I hacked into your e-mail account and read it.

373
00:16:45,810 --> 00:16:48,250
And... what did you think?

374
00:16:48,250 --> 00:16:50,490
Well, first, I think the fact that

375
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:52,520
you use your birthday as a password

376
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:54,100
is embarrassing.

377
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:57,670
Thank you.

378
00:16:59,050 --> 00:17:00,930
Second, um...

379
00:17:00,930 --> 00:17:03,700
I thought your paper was inspired.

380
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:07,370
Oh, well, that... that means a lot to me.

381
00:17:07,370 --> 00:17:09,040
Can I invite you in for tea?

382
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:10,820
Uh, no, no. I-I really...

383
00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:12,420
I have... I have to run.

384
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:14,100
Hey, Arthur. How are you?

385
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:16,490
Well, I guess one cup wouldn't hurt.

386
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:26,130
Oh, hey, buddy, what's up?

387
00:17:26,130 --> 00:17:28,710
Well, I was feeling bad about how I acted the other night

388
00:17:28,710 --> 00:17:31,400
so I made you a little something.

389
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:32,930
You didn't have to do that.

390
00:17:32,930 --> 00:17:33,910
Well, it was fun.

391
00:17:33,910 --> 00:17:36,440
I used some of the jewelry techniques you taught us.

392
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:41,590
It's a lightsaber belt buckle.

393
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:45,300
Wow, this is so cool, thanks.

394
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:47,370
Oh gets better.

395
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:53,900
And that's not even the best part. See?

396
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:56,230
I have one, too.

397
00:17:58,590 --> 00:17:59,750
Check it out.

398
00:17:59,750 --> 00:18:01,830
You can wear yours and we can have little sword fights

399
00:18:01,830 --> 00:18:04,090
whenever we want.

400
00:18:17,430 --> 00:18:20,270
Yeah, and my eighth favorite episode of Professor Proton

401
00:18:20,270 --> 00:18:22,320
was "Alka-Seltzer Rocket."

402
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:24,750
You said, "Plop, plop, fizz, fizz,

403
00:18:24,750 --> 00:18:26,780
oh, what a blast-off it is."

404
00:18:28,130 --> 00:18:29,400
Remember? Remember that?

405
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:30,780
Remember?

406
00:18:32,010 --> 00:18:32,840
He can't help it. He can't.

407
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:34,010
He really can't.

408
00:18:34,690 --> 00:18:35,330
You wait here.

409
00:18:35,330 --> 00:18:36,450
I'll get some Alka-Seltzer,

410
00:18:36,450 --> 00:18:37,590
we can build that rocket,

411
00:18:37,590 --> 00:18:39,830
and we can shoot it at Bill Nye's house.

412
00:18:41,290 --> 00:18:43,460
As long as I'm not within 500 feet of it.

413
00:18:48,790 --> 00:18:50,350
So, you, uh...

414
00:18:50,350 --> 00:18:52,270
you have... you have any...

415
00:18:52,270 --> 00:18:55,030
single grandmothers?

416
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:59,470
- Sorry, they're both married. - Good.

417
00:19:02,470 --> 00:19:05,700
H-Happily?

